1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,088
Wednesday, eleven at night.

2
00:00:02,138 --> 00:00:11,184
One of Malika's regular customers writes to her in a messenger: a birthday bouquet, delivered tomorrow at noon, can I pay now?

3
00:00:11,234 --> 00:00:13,320
They agree on the price in the chat.

4
00:00:13,370 --> 00:00:19,200
And many sellers like her have no shop site at all, and no Bekzod to build one.

5
00:00:19,250 --> 00:00:29,880
In Lesson 26, Bekzod's server created a payment and the buyer paid on the bank's page, and in Lesson 21, each website got its own limits.

6
00:00:29,930 --> 00:00:35,184
This lesson gives the same page to a seller with no website code at all.

7
00:00:35,234 --> 00:00:43,080
Writing a server, sealing requests and managing retries is out of reach for most small sellers.

8
00:00:43,130 --> 00:00:46,152
Sending a link in a chat is easy.

9
00:00:46,202 --> 00:00:48,384
But the link still has to be safe.

10
00:00:48,434 --> 00:00:50,712
It must open the real bank page.

11
00:00:50,762 --> 00:00:54,240
It must belong to a website the bank has checked.

12
00:00:54,290 --> 00:00:56,304
It must work only for a while.

13
00:00:56,354 --> 00:01:00,144
And its secret must not be readable from the bank's records.

14
00:01:00,194 --> 00:01:06,248
What can a seller send in a chat that is safe, and needs no code?

15
00:01:06,298 --> 00:01:08,120
That is a payment link.

16
00:01:08,170 --> 00:01:12,464
A merchant admin or operator makes one in the merchant portal.

17
00:01:12,514 --> 00:01:15,632
They pick one of the merchant's live websites.

18
00:01:15,682 --> 00:01:20,504
They write the amount, what the buyer pays for, and how long the link works.

19
00:01:20,554 --> 00:01:23,240
Behind the link is an ordinary payment.

20
00:01:23,290 --> 00:01:28,256
It simply has no API client, because no server created it.

21
00:01:28,306 --> 00:01:33,296
And it has no return address, because there is no shop page to go back to.

22
00:01:33,346 --> 00:01:35,576
Everything else is the same.

23
00:01:35,626 --> 00:01:38,696
The website's own limits still apply.

24
00:01:38,746 --> 00:01:42,008
The website's fee still applies.

25
00:01:42,058 --> 00:01:49,160
The link is the bank's page address with a one-time page token, just like the link Bekzod's server receives.

26
00:01:49,210 --> 00:01:52,280
The bank keeps only a fingerprint of that token.

27
00:01:52,330 --> 00:01:56,384
So the full link is shown once, at the moment it is made.

28
00:01:56,434 --> 00:02:01,280
The merchant chooses how long it works, up to the bank's chosen maximum.

29
00:02:01,330 --> 00:02:07,472
The buyer pays on the same bank page as any other payment, with the same card frame.

30
00:02:07,522 --> 00:02:11,744
And a link nobody pays in time is closed as expired.

31
00:02:11,794 --> 00:02:15,176
I like this feature because it adds almost nothing new.

32
00:02:15,226 --> 00:02:21,104
A link is just a payment without a server, and every old rule still guards it.

33
00:02:21,154 --> 00:02:24,440
In the merchant portal, she opens payment links.

34
00:02:24,490 --> 00:02:31,472
The page says it plainly: sell in a chat, and send a buyer a link to the bank's payment page.

35
00:02:31,522 --> 00:02:33,344
She presses new link.

36
00:02:33,394 --> 00:02:36,008
The form asks for a live website.

37
00:02:36,058 --> 00:02:42,224
If none of her websites took payments yet, the form would say so, and nothing could be made.

38
00:02:42,274 --> 00:02:46,928
She picks her website, types what the buyer pays for, and the amount.

39
00:02:46,978 --> 00:02:50,144
She chooses how many hours the link works.

40
00:02:50,194 --> 00:02:54,368
Ask for longer than the bank allows, and the bank refuses it.

41
00:02:54,418 --> 00:02:56,240
She presses create.

42
00:02:56,290 --> 00:02:59,048
A window says your link is ready.

43
00:02:59,098 --> 00:03:05,288
It explains that the link is shown only once, and the bank keeps only its fingerprint.

44
00:03:05,338 --> 00:03:08,552
She presses copy link, and it says copied.

45
00:03:08,602 --> 00:03:10,544
She pastes it into the chat.

46
00:03:10,594 --> 00:03:15,536
Back on the list, the new link waits for the buyer, without its address.

47
00:03:15,586 --> 00:03:20,816
A teammate who only views can see this list, but has no new link button.

48
00:03:20,866 --> 00:03:23,504
Now the buyer taps the link in the chat.

49
00:03:23,554 --> 00:03:29,240
The same secure bank page opens, with the website, what it is for, and the amount.

50
00:03:29,290 --> 00:03:33,440
The buyer pays through the card frame, and the page says paid.

51
00:03:33,490 --> 00:03:37,664
There is no back to the shop link, because there is no shop page.

52
00:03:37,714 --> 00:03:41,192
In Malika's portal, the link now shows paid.

53
00:03:41,242 --> 00:03:43,616
Another link is never paid.

54
00:03:43,666 --> 00:03:49,208
It turns expired, and a buyer who opens it sees that the link has expired.

55
00:03:49,258 --> 00:03:54,680
So a seller without a website gets the same safe page as everyone else.

56
00:03:54,730 --> 00:03:57,896
The card never touches the chat or Malika.

57
00:03:57,946 --> 00:04:02,312
A leaked copy of the bank's records holds no working links.

58
00:04:02,362 --> 00:04:09,008
And the website's limits still guard every link, so a link cannot become a back door.

59
00:04:09,058 --> 00:04:15,056
To recap: a payment link is a payment without a server, on the same bank page.

60
00:04:15,106 --> 00:04:20,440
So what guards a link, when no server created it?

61
00:04:20,490 --> 00:04:26,224
Every old rule: the page token, the expiry, and the website's limits.

62
00:04:26,274 --> 00:04:28,288
Next: giving money back.
